From 6b39076f7070610ca8fd8220c0eb7d173c31b867 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: samhenrigold <49251320+samhenrigold@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 22:49:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] TIGER: make the IPC wire format identical in a 32-bit and a
 64-bit process

Prepares the message set for the split where a 32-bit UI process talks to a
64-bit content process.

The socket framing header, IPC::MessageInfo, was three size_t fields and a bool
blitted raw onto the socket: 12 bytes on i386 and 24 on x86_64, which would
desynchronise the stream at the first message. It is now fixed-width with
explicit padding and static_asserts on size, alignment and every offset. The
new struct is 12 bytes on both, so the 32-bit side's framing is unchanged.

Two message fields were size_t and are now uint32_t: SnapOffset::snapAreaIndices
indexes the snap areas on a page and PlatformXR::LayerInfo::numImages counts
swapchain images, so neither is near 2^32, and every consumer already widens
them.

The arithmetic argument coder now rejects long, unsigned long and long double,
which covers size_t, ptrdiff_t and uintptr_t as they are spelled on Darwin, so a
future field of one of those types fails to compile instead of corrupting
messages. int64_t and uint64_t are long long on Darwin and are unaffected.

Beyond that, alignment turned out to be on the wire too, which an audit of field
types alone does not catch. Encoder::encodeSpan pads the stream to alignof(T)
and Decoder::decodeSpan seeks to the same boundary, and alignof is 4 for
uint64_t, int64_t and double on Darwin i386 against 8 on x86_64. A message of
{ uint32_t, uint64_t, uint32_t, double } lays out at 0, 4, 12, 16 for 24 bytes
on i386 and at 0, 8, 16, 24 for 32 bytes on x86_64. Nothing has to be declared
wrongly for that: a uint64_t is enough, and uint64_t is what every container
length and every ObjectIdentifier already uses. Both sides now pad by
wireAlignmentOf, which gives 8-byte scalars an alignment of 8 everywhere.

